\cf2 \cb5 I checked the toroidal reference. And there's a lovely coincidence I didn't anticipate.\cf0 \cb1 \

\f1\b \cf2 \cb5 Hans Reichenbach actually formulated something called 
\f3\i Theorem \uc0\u952 
\f1\i0  (theta).
\f0\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 It concerns the relationship between geometry, physical measurement, and hypothetical universal forces. In 
\f4\i The Philosophy of Space and Time
\f0\i0 , he also considers a toroidal space and the peculiar consequences of trying to describe one topology using another.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 The torus example leads directly into his discussion of 
\f1\b causal anomalies
\f0\b0 : apparent correspondences between physically distinct locations that cannot simply be explained by ordinary causal signals. \cf0 \cb1 \

