\cf2 \cb5 And I think the deeper recognition is this:\cf0 \cb1 \

\f1\b \cf2 \cb5 The identity of an event, the coordinates assigned to it, and the causal relationships connecting it to other events are three different things.
\f0\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 A representation can change without changing the underlying event. Two apparently different representations can refer to one event. Two apparently identical observations can refer to different events. And none of those correspondences, by themselves, establishes a causal connection.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 Reichenbach's broader program treated causal relationships and the conventions connecting measurements to physical objects as foundational problems in constructing spacetime descriptions. \cf0 \cb1 \

