\cf2 \cb5 This means something quite striking:\cf0 \cb1 \

\f1\b \cf2 \cb5 Even complete knowledge of the local metric derivatives at every individual point need not distinguish those two global spaces.
\f0\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 That's stronger than simply saying the third derivative adds information the first derivative lacked.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 In general relativity, the connection involves first derivatives of the metric; curvature involves second derivatives. Further derivatives can characterize changes in curvature. But those differential quantities alone don't settle global topology. \cf0 \cb1 \

