\cf2 \cb5 And now we have an unusually precise analogy to your dataset architecture.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 A collection of isolated QKV experience rows can contain excellent local information about what happened, what was anticipated, and what followed.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 But the global organization also depends on 
\f1\b how those local experiences are identified, connected, and composed
\f0\b0 .\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 One day can refer back to another. A representation can be encountered again. An action can affect a later observation. Multiple observations can describe the same object without becoming the same observation.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 Those are not merely additional scalar properties attached to individual rows.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 They're relationships among rows.\cf0 \cb1 \
