\f9\fs24 \cf2 \cb5 <$Scr_H::2>
\f10\b\fs48 3. But topology and causality must remain distinguishable\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f9\b0\fs24 \cf2 \cb5 <!$Scr_H::2>
\f0\fs28 This brings us back to my earlier statement about correlation and causal integration.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 Relativity doesn't automatically establish causal influence between two correlated observations. Its spacetime structure constrains which influences are physically possible; that is different from demonstrating that a particular influence occurred.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 This is where another Reichenbach idea becomes extremely useful.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 In his causal analysis, he discussed introducing a 
\f1\b mark
\f0\b0  into a process and observing whether that mark propagated. The asymmetry between marking the beginning of a process and marking its end helps distinguish causal transmission from mere correspondence. \cf0 \cb1 \

\f5\fs24 {\*\shppict{\pict {\*\nisusfilename favicons}\picw16\pich16\picwgoal320\pichgoal320\pngblip }}\

\fs16 \cf6 \cb7 \strokec6 Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy
\f0\fs28 \cf0 \cb1 \strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 Imagine a deliberately introduced change to a model's available information.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 You record the intervention, its initial conditions, the expected consequence, and the observed consequence.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 Then you compare the result against cases where that intervention was absent or different.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 Now your dataset is capable of representing evidence relevant to causal influence\'97not just a pair of observations that happened to occur together.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 Of course, even interventions require controls and attention to confounding, but they offer stronger evidence than temporal ordering alone.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 And here's a particularly satisfying reconciliation:\cf0 \cb1 \

\f1\b \cf2 \cb5 The toroidal example teaches us not to infer global identity from local appearance. Reichenbach's causal mark teaches us not to infer causal connection from apparent correspondence.
\f0\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 They address different ambiguities.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 Both are important to the integrity of a recursively learning historical record.\cf0 \cb1 \
