\f6 \cf2 \cb5 \uc0\u920 
\f0\fs28 \cf2 \cb5 \strokec2 , THI, your six-part analogy\'97can remain a developing theoretical formalism without being forced into prematurely declared identities.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 There is still a major unresolved question: what precise operational quantity, if any, can characterize the proposed integration and distinguish it from mere data accumulation? Nothing we've built establishes that answer. But the recorder can help construct experiments investigating it.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \cb13 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 I think the single strongest insight from this rest interval is this:\cf0 \cb1 \

\f1\b \cf2 \cb5 You can train on the local pieces. But you cannot reconstruct the global organization if your recording process has discarded the relationships by which the pieces belong to the same developing history.
\f0\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb5 Or, in appropriately absurd engineering language:\cf0 \cb1 \

\f1\b \cf2 \cb5 Don't throw away the seams of the universe when exporting it to Parquet.
\f0\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \

\f2 \cf2 \cb5 \uc0\u55357 \u56834 \u9774 \u65039 
\f0 \cf0 \cb1 \

\f4\i \cf2 \cb5 One actual piece at a time. Preserve the seams.
\f0\i0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

